<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
{% include "_head.html.j2" %}
<title>{{ page.title|default('Queries') }} &mdash; queries</title>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
  <nav class="nav">
    <a href="../index.html">Collection</a>
    <a href="index.html" class="active">Queries</a>
    <a href="../map.html">Map</a>
  </nav>

  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>{{ page.title|default('Query this collection') }}</h1>
    {% if page.intro %}<p class="intro">{{ page.intro }}</p>{% endif %}
    <p class="note">
    {% if engine == 'pyodide' %}
      Every query below runs in your browser: the graph is fetched as a static
      Turtle file and queried with rdflib under Pyodide. There is no endpoint,
      nothing you type leaves your machine, and an archived copy of this
      repository stays queryable with no service to keep alive. The first page
      you open downloads roughly 10&nbsp;MB of Python runtime; after that the
      browser has it cached.
    {% else %}
      Every query below runs against the NFDI4Objects Knowledge Graph endpoint,
      scoped to this collection.
    {% endif %}
    </p>
  </header>

  <ul class="qlist">
{% for q in queries %}
    <li>
      <a class="title" href="{{ q.id }}.html">{{ q.title }}</a>
      <p>{{ q.blurb }}</p>
      <p class="meta">
        <span class="tag">{{ q.view_label }}</span>
        <span class="tag">{{ q.rows_at_build }} rows when built</span>
        <a href="rq/{{ q.id }}.rq">{{ q.id }}.rq</a>
      </p>
    </li>
{% endfor %}
  </ul>

  <footer class="page-foot">
    <p>
      All of them on one page: <a href="all.html">all.html</a>. The plain
      <code>.rq</code> files are in <code>rq/</code>, and the graph itself is
      <a href="{{ graph.page_url }}">{{ graph.url }}</a>{% if graph.megabytes %}
      ({{ graph.megabytes }}&nbsp;MB){% endif %}. The same queries are in
      <a href="../metadata.ttl">metadata.ttl</a> as
      <code>sh:SPARQLSelectExecutable</code>, where a harvester can read them.
    </p>
  </footer>
</div>
</body>
</html>
